First, notice that $5! = 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2! \, .$ Then, we can cancel the $2!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:

$$
\require{cancel}
	\eqalign{
\dfrac {5!} {2!} 
&= \dfrac {5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2!} {2!} \[5pt]

&= \dfrac {5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot  \cancel{2!}} {\cancel{2!}} \[5pt]
&= 		\dfrac {5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3} {1} \[5pt]
&= 
		60
	}
$$
